A||N||9||Haze_Performance||In need of some Spring Info..||||10||
Z||000000||Haze_Performance||04-27-2000||03:16 AM||||Im looking to change the springs in my 85. I know Eibach sells them but what are some other good brands and prices? Anyone have lowering springs in there car? How does the car ride? I do ALOT of expressway driving (80mph+) so im looking for a slight lowering all around. I dont want to be scraping the ground. ||||reg||10
Z||000001||80's BOY||04-27-2000||09:04 AM||||on my 85 gt, i shopped around along time. call Bob Steiger from twin lakes fiero parts. he usually has a set sitting around. as for options, Eibach and maybe Ground Control are the only two companies that i know of. Ryan Motorsports or the Fiero Store will have some for sure. how low do you want it? 1 inch wasn't enough for me, so i cut 1 1/2 coils off the lowering springs. looks AWESOME.....but it rides like a brick on a skateboard. coilovers are probably next. be sure to get a 4 wheel alignment after the lowering job or you will chew up your tires in one trip. this is a given when altering any suspension parts. good luck!!||||reg||6
Z||000002||Haze_Performance||04-28-2000||06:33 PM||||Thanks 80's<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Haze_Performance:<BR><B>Im looking to change the springs in my 85. I know Eibach sells them but what are some other good brands and prices? Anyone have lowering springs in there car? How does the car ride? I do ALOT of expressway driving (80mph+) so im looking for a slight lowering all around. I dont want to be scraping the ground. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Spyder||04-28-2000||07:41 PM||||I used the Suspension Techniques lowering springs.  I like them. Got them from JC Whitney.  Couldn’t tell you an overall difference because I changed out the ball joints, poly bushings, sway bar, Koni shocks. <BR>You can cut the ones you have down.<BR>Half a coil equals about an inch.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||04-28-2000||07:51 PM||||Do you cut from the top or the bottom?  Seems like a fairly cheap way to lower a car at least temporarily.  I plan on doing the whole suspension thing in the future (too much $$ now), but I'd like to lower the car in the meantime.  If I lowered the car a couple of inches (by cutting the springs) would I need different shocks/struts or could I go with new OEM?||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||04-29-2000||03:34 PM||||Your car will hit the rubber bumpstops if you just cut the stock coils.In order to get a similar spring rate as aftermarket lowering springs when cutting your stock ones,you'll need to use a spacer to get it back up into the range you want.Or just be willing to live with a car that bounces around and have people look at you when you go driving around,and also it'll be worse/dangerous at higher speeds when the car hits the bumpstops and loads the tires...<P>Before I lowered my car a little,I just made a screen saver with a lowered Fiero until I could do it right.<BR>||||reg||13
Z||000006||Monkeyman||04-29-2000||06:49 PM||||Let me get this straight.  I can cut, say, 2 inches off of my stock springs, but then I have to put, say, a 2" spacer under my springs so I don't hit the bump stops?  What's the point?  I'll just take some pics of the Grape Ape and edit them until I can discuss this with JC Whitney.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ed||04-29-2000||09:00 PM||||We cut a few of our locals members cars' springs one turn with a gas torch.  There were apparently no ill effects.  <P>I say apparently because I didn't cut mine, but I never heard anyone complain.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||04-29-2000||11:39 PM||||Well it's more like hack it to three inches of drop,then add a spacer to get it off the bumpstops,and back up to the 1-1/2"-lowered mark.<BR>Not really worth it unless you've got free machine shop services and some time to kill.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||04-30-2000||02:35 AM||||I have the Suspension Techniques springs from JC Whitney, they'll drop the front about 1-1/2" to 2". The Eibach are supposedly a slightly better ride (I think they're progressive spring rate) and maybe not quite as much of a drop as the S/T springs, but they also cost more.||||reg||1
